e → final :llm synthesis. The final :llm integrates the aggregated per-chunk material into a cohesive narrative answer. Choose it when the answer requires reasoning across the whole document AND the output is a synthesis (briefing, summary, recommendation), not a flat extraction. Distinct from MapReduce (which uses :code for deterministic mechanical reduction) and from ChunkedExtraction (whose terminal step is the :aggregate merge). Watch the synthesis token budget — aggressive per-chunk extraction can overflow the final context — and pair with a grounding judge if claims must be traceable.\n\n\n#### Alternative #1 — Briefing generation (confidence: 0.92)\nWhy this fits: This is a specialized version of ResearchThenSynthesize. It fits the need to identify 'cross-chunk patterns' (like income/expense trends in CSVs) to produce a cohesive narrative (the forecast). The requirement for a table and explicit assumptions aligns well with the synthesis stage's ability to reason across all per-chunk extractions.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nBriefing generation is the canonical ResearchThenSynthesize instance: chunk → per-chunk :llm extraction → :aggregate → final synthesis :llm that produces a cohesive narrative. The synthesis stage's access to ALL per-chunk material enables cross-chunk reasoning (themes, contradictions). Always extract supporting quotes alongside summaries to support traceability. Watch context overflow — reduce per-chunk granularity if chunk count is high. Wire a grounding judge on the output when the briefing goes to a decision-maker. Use document-analysis (chunked-extraction) for structured triples; use chunked-extraction for terminal aggregation without synthesis.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence of :chunk-document → :map-each :llm (per-chunk extraction) → :aggregate (merged material) → :llm synthesizer (cohesive narrative)\n  - natural for any 'read X long material and produce a Y-paragraph briefing' work — executive briefings, research summaries, situation reports\n  - outputs are typically a single coherent text artifact organized by topic/section, not a structured key-value extraction\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** the synthesis stage's :llm has access to ALL per-chunk extracted material in :reads — produces a narrative that reasons across chunks rather than concatenating per-chunk summaries (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the briefing needs to identify cross-chunk patterns (recurring themes, contradictions, progression of an argument)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:chunk-document {:from :source :size 4000 :into :chunks}] [:map-each {:from :chunks :as :chunk :into :extracts :max-concurrency 3} [:llm {:reads [:chunk] :writes [:key-points :quotes]}]] [:aggregate {:from :extracts :writes [:all-points :all-quotes]}] [:llm {:reads [:all-points :all-quotes :briefing-prompt] :writes [:briefing]}] [:final {:keys [:briefing]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** extract specific QUOTES alongside summaries in the per-chunk step — the synthesis stage can then ground claims to source quotes, supporting traceability (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the briefing audience may verify claims against source material AND traceability matters\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:chunk] :writes [:key-points :supporting-quotes] :output-schemas {:key-points [:vector :string] :supporting-quotes [:vector :string]}}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** the synthesis stage's context can overflow when per-chunk extracts are too rich AND the chunk count is high — synthesizer truncates or hallucinates connections (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: per-chunk extraction emits >500 tokens per chunk AND chunk count is >15\n    - Recommended fix: either reduce per-chunk extraction granularity (key-points-only, no quotes) OR add an intermediate :code stage that selects top-K per-chunk extracts before synthesis\n\n  - **Failure mode:** without a grounding judge, the synthesis stage can fabricate connections between per-chunk extracts that aren't actually in the source — a 'plausible narrative' that doesn't survive verification (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the briefing is going to a decision-maker AND no grounding check is wired downstream\n    - Recommended fix: wire a grounding judge on the briefing output that checks each claim against the source quotes — catches fabricated connections before the briefing ships\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - executive briefing from a long research report: chunked extraction → final cohesive 3-paragraph briefing\n  - situation-report generation from a stream of incident logs: per-incident extraction → final narrative summary\n  - literature review summary: chunked paper extraction → final synthesized review section\n\nAssigned to domain child 190629bc-dd40-3362-a1af-be65bab9779a (label: nonprofit-financial-forecasting) under the top match — this campaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\nCapabilities:\n  - Domain family born for a task whose subject matter, material and output kind are: The task involves financial forecasting and risk analysis across multiple data points; while the candidate describes narrative synthesis, it specifically handles the 'reasoning across a whole document' and 'synthesis' requirements for the forecast and sensitivity analysis.\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - INSTRUCTION:\n\nGenerate 12-month cash-flow forecast for nonprofit starting Jan. Use attached CSVs for historical income/expense data. Include £5,000 monthly overhead and £50,000 grant expected in March. Apply 20% reduction to private donations for summer months. Output must be a table in GBP. Must include sensitivity analysis for 10% utility cost increase and 15% fundraising shortfall. Explicitly list all assumptions and identify top three operational risks to liquid reserves. Exclude tax filings and long-term investment strategy. Urgent.\n\nREADS: (none)\nWRITES: :result\nMCP-TOOLS: (none)\nBROWSER-TOOLS: (none)\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 1 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Design (confidence: 0.95)\n   Why this fits: The 'Design' candidate is the strongest fit because the task requires producing a structured 12-month cash-flow plan (a 'committable plan/spec') from specific inputs and constraints. The instruction explicitly asks for assumptions and risks, which matches this candidate's recommended-pattern of outputting a :map with named sections for 'decisions' (assumptions) and 'risks'.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Design produces a single committable plan/spec/architecture from inputs (goal + constraints + optional candidate options). Always structure as :map with named sections (decisions / open-questions / risks) — downstream automated stages bind on these. For non-trivial designs with interacting decisions, iterate via draft → critique → refined-spec. ALWAYS require an :open-questions field — empty means 'I'm confident', non-empty surfaces the gap before downstream stages run against an implicit guess. Composes into iterative-refinement (draft → critique → finalize) and sequential-pipeline (analysis → design → code-building) shells. Avoid when the task is option generation (use ideation) or execution against a known plan (use code-building).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given a goal + constraints + (optionally) candidate options, produce a single concrete plan/spec/architecture as the chosen direction\n  - natural fit as the convergence stage after research/extraction/analysis/ideation; the gate before code-building or execution\n  - outputs are typically a single structured spec (sections / decisions / open-questions) with explicit decisions rather than a list of options\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** structure the design output as :map with named sections (decisions / open-questions / risks) — downstream code-building or execution can :read specific sections without re-parsing prose (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the design will feed automated downstream stages (code-building, validation, execution)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:goal :constraints :options] :writes [:spec] :output-schemas {:spec [:map [:decisions [:vector :string]] [:open-questions [:vector :string]] [:risks [:vector :string]]]}}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** iterate design via draft → critique → refined-spec when the design is non-trivial — single-pass design tends to leave decisions implicit (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the design has multiple interacting decisions AND the cost of a wrong call is high\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:goal] :writes [:draft-spec]}] [:llm {:reads [:draft-spec :goal] :writes [:critique]}] [:llm {:reads [:draft-spec :critique] :writes [:refined-spec]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** designs that leave open-questions implicit force the downstream stage to either fail or guess — both produce drift between intended and actual outcome (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: downstream stages will execute against the design\n    - Recommended fix: require an explicit :open-questions field in the spec output — empty means 'I'm confident'; non-empty surfaces the gap\n\n  - **Failure mode:** design without explicit consideration of the constraints (as part of :reads) tends to produce specs that look reasonable but violate the constraints in subtle ways (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the task has hard constraints (budget / API limits / shape requirements)\n    - Recommended fix: always include constraints as a separate :reads input on the design stage AND require the spec output to reference how each constraint was honored\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - technical architecture spec from a problem brief + constraints\n  - experiment design from a research question + dataset constraints\n  - API contract design from a feature spec + backwards-compat requirements\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n               