ed example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:chunk-document {:from :document :size 5000 :into :chunks}] [:map-each {:from :chunks :as :chunk :into :all-results :max-concurrency 3} [:llm {:reads [:chunk] :writes [:items]}]] [:aggregate {:from :all-results :writes [:items]}] [:llm {:reads [:document :all-results] :writes [:summary]}] [:final {:keys [:all-results :summary]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** unbounded :max-concurrency on the :map-each exhausts sub-LLM rate limits on documents with 6+ chunks (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: input is a document with >6 chunks AND :max-concurrency is unset OR set higher than provider rate-limit tolerance\n    - Recommended fix: always set :max-concurrency to 3 on the :map-each parent (safe default across major LLM providers); tune higher only after measuring against the specific provider's rate limit\n\n  - **Failure mode:** skipping the :aggregate step means downstream consumers see a raw vector of per-iteration maps — usually not the shape they want (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the tree has :map-each but no :aggregate AND a downstream node expects per-key flat data\n    - Recommended fix: always include :aggregate after :map-each (or use :code with explicit merge logic for non-default consolidation)\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - foundation pattern for any large-document extraction task (document analysis, risk analysis, legal issue detection)\n  - per-page processing of multi-page documents (image, PDF, or structured page-separated content)\n  - any task where the input is too large for a single :llm context window AND the extraction is per-chunk independent\n\nAssigned to domain child 9abeb488-132e-3bb3-aba0-779b502b3654 (label: engineering-standup-summary) under the top match — this campaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\nCapabilities:\n  - Domain family born for a task whose subject matter, material and output kind are: The candidate names 'briefing' and 'summary' as representative output kinds, which directly matches the 'dev log' and 'executive summary' requirements of the task.\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - INSTRUCTION:\n\nPlease review the transcript I’ve uploaded from this morning’s 20-minute Solaris engineering standup. The audio was pretty chaotic due to the discussion on the API migration, so I need you to synthesize it into a clean dev log using these steps:\n\n1. Draft a three-sentence executive summary tailored for the two team members out sick. This should strictly cover the current build status and the single primary blocker identified during the call.\n2. Extract all finalized technical decisions. I only want the \"done deals\"—specifically architectural choices or PR approvals that were officially greenlit. Ignore any open brainstorming or circular debates.\n3. Build an accountability log for all action items. Each entry must clearly link the owner (e.g., 'Dave' or 'Frontend Team') to their specific task and the mentioned deadline. If a deadline wasn't explicitly stated, label it as \"TBD.\"\n4. Compile a \"Parking Lot\" section for any technical hurdles, edge cases, or bugs that were raised but intentionally deferred to a later deep-dive session or the next sprint.\n\nThe final output needs to be high-density, professional, and ready to paste directly into our Slack channel. Avoid any \"play-by-play\" commentary or fluff.\n\nREADS: (none)\nWRITES: :result\nMCP-TOOLS: (none)\nBROWSER-TOOLS: (none)\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 1 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Synthesis (confidence: 0.95)\n   Why this fits: The core of this task is 'synthesizing' a chaotic engineering standup transcript into a 'clean dev log' with specific sections (summary, decisions